The '''Lepidocystidae''' (sometimes misspelled '''Lepidocyst''o''idae''', not to be confused with the class Lepidocystoid''ea'' that was proposed at the same time but later abandoned) are an extinct family that constitue the most [[basal (phylogenetics)|basal]] known group within the [[paraphyletic]] class "[[Eocrinoidea]]", and therefore within the subphylum [[Blastozoa]] as a whole.  Its members, along with the transitional [[Felbabkacystidae]], are also informally known as "imbricates" or "imbricate Eocrinoids"<ref>{{harvnb|Mooie|Lefebvre|Guensburg|Nohejovlá|Dupichaud|2024}}</ref> after the likewise paraphyletic order Imbricata.<ref>{{harvnb|Mooi|Lefebvre|Guensburg|Nohejlová|Dupichaud|2024|page=480}}</ref>

== Description ==

Lepidocystids are unusual among blastozoans in that, aside from the relatively flat upward-facing oral surface, the entire body wall is covered with imbricate plates, including a wide stalk that is not well-differentiated from the main body.  The oral surface, which contains, has tessalate plating with epispires for respiration.  The oral surface contains the mouth and other openings, as well as the ambulacral grooves which support brachioles, the feeding structures that define the blastozoan clade.

In more derived blastozoans, the main body, or theca, is composed of tessalate plates, with the imbricate area reduced to a well-differentiated stalk.  The transition from the primative lepidocystid condition to the more derived condition is illustrated by the [[Felbabkacystidae]].


Like most blastozoans, lepidocystids are [[filter feeders]] that attach to a substrate and have feeding appendages known as [[brachioles]].  The oral surface is flat, and consists of [[tessalate]] plates, while the body is made of imbricate (overlapping) plates, with a relatively thick stalk that is not well-differentiated from the main body.

==Classification==

===History===

''Lepidocystis wanneri'' from the [[Kinzers Formation]] ([[Cambrian Stage 4]]) was described by [[August Foerste]] in a posthumous publication in 1938 and assigned to the class Eocrinoidea.  In the 1967 [[Treatise on Invertebrate Paleontology]], Durham noted differences between ''Lepidocystis'' and other Eocrinoidea, as well as a few similarities to the Edrioasteroidea, and deemed it sufficiently distinctive to erect the family Lepidocystidae and the class Lepidocystoidea.<ref>{{harvnb|Durham|1967}}</ref>

While erecting the subphylum Blastozoa in 1973, James Sprinkle examined additional, more complete specimens of both ''Lepidocystis'' and a newly described genus and species, ''Kinzercystis durhami'', all also from the Kinzers Formation.  These specimens demonstrated more affiliation with other eocrinoids than had been apparent in the specimens available to Durham, leading Sprinkle to reject Lepidocystoidea and replace it with the basal eocrinoid order Imbricata.

In 1990, Fatka and Kordule described the new genus and species ''Vyscystis ubaghsi'' to the Lepidocydtidae, without changing Sprinkle's class- and order-level assignments.  [TODO: Nardin ''et al.'' dispense with Eocrinoidea and Imbricata].  However, when reviewing the phylogentic sygnificance of ''Vyscystis'' in 2019, Nohejlová ''et al.'' [TODO: they just accepted Nardin ''et al.'']


===Durham (Lepidocystoidea)===

* free-living placoid echinoderms with differentiated oral and aboral surfaces
* oral surface with numerous small plates with sutural pores
* oral surface with numerous free biserial arms [note: per Sprinkle this describes true brachioles]
* aboral surface with numerous imbricating plates originating at apex of surface
* anus marginal on oral surface, with well-developed anal pyramid
* arms grooved with cover plates
* arms arranged in concentric circlets on oral surface, oldest outermost
* arms resting on basal flat plates
* arms presumably for feeding
* organs in sutural pores presumably respiratory

Lepidocystoids differ from:
* Eocrinoids:
** restriction of sutural pore-bearing surface to the interbrachial oral surface
** the presence of imbricating plates on the aboral surface
** the circlets of arms on the oral surface
** the mode of attachment of the arms
* Edrioasteroids, Cystoids, and Helicoplacoids:
** presence of sutural pores
* Camptostromatoids:
** possession of only a single type of plate in any area
** the position of the anus on the oral surface [''Camptostroma'' now described with anus on oral surface]
* ''Stromatocystites'' (which has sutural pores on the oral surface)
** presence of free arms

===Sprinkle (Imbricata)===

* early eocrinoids
* strongly differentiated calyx
* both imbricate and adjacent calyx plates
* aboral portion of conical calyx and cylindrical holdfast composed of imbricate plates lacking epispires
* oral surface composed of adjacent plates having numerous epispires
* oral surface plates bearing the ambulacral system with 5 symmetrical food grooves
* ambulacral food grooves have numerous true brachioles

Lists Durham's distinctions as:
* circlets of arms
** attached alternately along both sides of each lateral groove, branching, not circlets
* mode of arm attachment
** true brachioles with typical attachment
* presence of imbricate plates on the aboral surface
** '''only remaining distinction'''
* restriction of epispire-bearing are to the oral surface
** ''Kinzercystis'' epispire surface much like Gogiid calyx

===Fatka (Vyscystis)===

* Eocrinoids with irregular multiplated cylindrical to conical theca
* aboral portion of theca (= calyx and holdfast) composed of adorally imbricate plates without epispires
* oral surface composed of adjacent plates bearing large epispires
* Ambulacral system confined to oral surface, composed of unbranched biserial brachioles, probably five in number


** ''Lepidocysts'' more different but only because oral surface is smaller
* free-living or embedded in sediment rather than attached by a stem
** "distinctive" cylindrical holdfast discovered in additional specimens

Not a class because several other classes including echinoids, edrioasteroids, and stylophorans have partial imbricate plating combined with the "more usual" adjacent plating.
Both Lepidocystidae and its then-containing class Lepidocystoidea were erected by Durham in 1967, with ''Lepidocystes'' as the sole genus.<ref>{{harvnb|Durham|1967}}</ref>  Durham justified the class-level distinction by noting several differences between ''Lepidocystites'' and both eocrinoids and edrioasteroids, but by the time the ph
Lepidocystidae was erected by Durham in 1967 as the sole taxon in his newly-erected class Lepidocystoidea, of which it was the sole member.  The only lepidocystid genus known at the time was ''[[Lepidocystis]]'', which had originally been assigned to the class Eocrinoidea by Foerste upon its discovery in 1938.  

===Nardin ''et al.'' (direct under Blastozoa)===

* 'Calyx-bearing' blastozoans
* Oral disc made of numerous adjacent plates with simple sutural pores
* anal pyramid and hydropore-gonopore located in 'CD' interradius in the oral disc
* ambulacral system confined to oral disc
** central covered mouth
** several radially arranged ambulacral groves embedded into the oral surface
** numerous straight or coiled biserial brachioles alternately arranged alongside each groove
* aboral cup
** cone-shaped or elongate into a cylindrical stalk
** composed of numerous imbricate plates

Noted that Imbricata diagnosed by well-marked differentiation between tesselate oral surface / imbricate aboral, however:
* several other echinoderms exhibit a comperable combination of imbricate and tesselate platings
** ''Camptostroma''
** several edrioasteroids
** primitive solutans
** ''Felbabkacystis''

Therefore Imbricata was mostly described on plesiomorphic characters (e.g. imbricate plating, sutural pores) and not considered a valid taxonomic entity either (same as Eocrinoidea - essentially they are saying it is paraphyletic despite Lepidocystidae being monophyletic).

Lepidocystids have only one apomorphy of Blastozoa (presence of brachioles) but lack theca, spout-like oral area, holomeric stem).  Their bipartite body-wall organization recalls camptostromatoids, baal crinoids, edrioasteroids, etc.

* Introduce "calyx" for body wall of lepidocystids.

===History of Classification===

This taxon was originally erected as the class Lepidocystoidea by Durham in 1967 in recognition of the distinctiveness of ''Lepidocystis'', the only genus known at the time.  In his 1973 publication erecting the subphylum Blastozoa, James Sprinkle demoted Lepidocystoidea to the family Lepidocystidae, and assigned it to his newly erected eocrinoidean order Imbricata.  Recent authors have assigned the lepidocystids directly to Blastozoa on the grounds that Eocrinoidea is paraphyletic, but retained the family ranking rather than re-promoting it to a class.<ref>{{harvnb|Wang|Nohejovlá|Sun|Zeng|Lefebvre|Yang|Zhao|2024|p=3}}</ref>  However, the same authors continue to use "eocrinoids" as an informal term including lepidocystids,<ref>{{harvnb|Wang|Nohejovlá|Sun|Zeng|Lefebvre|Yang|Zhao|2024|p=8}}</ref>   and other authors refer to the group as "imbricates,"<ref>{{harvnb|Mooi|Lefebvre|Guensburg|Nohejovlá|Dupichaud|2024}}</ref> keeping the older class- and order-level terminology in use informally.

Despite appearing later in the fossil record than the more common [[Gogia|gogiid]] eocrinoids, lepidocystids are believed to be the most basal blastozoans.  The [[Felbabkacystidae]] are transitional forms showing that gogiids derived from lepidocystid-like ancestors:

